# Gopher Eats

A pizza delivery demo built with GoForj's Vue starter kit and three independent
Apps. Place an order, watch the kitchen prepare and bake it, then follow a gopher
courier to your doorstep. Every tracker update comes from a real backend event.

## Start your shift

From the project root:

```sh
forj dev
```

The generated development loop starts the local services, builds the Vue UI and
all three Go Apps, and runs them together. Open `http://localhost:3000`.
Grafana is at `http://localhost:13001`, with local credentials `admin` / `admin`.

Choose a pizza and place an order. **Burn the first pizza** injects one oven
failure so the cooking job retries. **Take the scenic route** slows the courier.
**Lunch rush** places three orders through the same kitchen worker. Use the order
tabs to switch trackers while the others keep moving.

**View trace in Grafana** opens that order's actual Tempo trace. Use the logs icon
on a span to inspect the service's contextual logs in Loki. Early stages can
appear before all spans have finished exporting; refresh the trace after delivery.

## App boundaries

| App | Default HTTP port | Responsibility |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `app` (storefront) | 3000 | Vue UI, order acceptance, progress history, SSE |
| `kitchen` | 3002 | HTTP handoff, cooking queue producer and worker |
| `delivery` | 3001 | Ready-event consumer, courier progress publication |

The storefront calls the kitchen using `inspects.Manager.HTTPClient`. The kitchen
dispatches `eats.cook` on its app-scoped NATS queue and publishes `eats.order.ready`
when the pizza is cooked. Delivery consumes that event and broadcasts progress.
The storefront subscribes to `eats.order.progress` and streams snapshots to the
browser. Snapshots survive an SSE reconnect and tolerate event reordering.

The browser's tracker uses business events. Trace context crosses HTTP, queue,
and event boundaries through the normal framework instrumentation. The order's
trace ID is used only to build the Grafana link.

## Where to look

- `cmd/app/frontend/src/App.vue`: reactive tracker, order selection, SSE lifecycle.
- `cmd/app/frontend/src/eats/`: typed history model and the neighborhood scene.
- `cmd/app/frontend/src/eats/KitchenScene.vue`: distinct received, preparing,
  baking, retrying, and pickup scenes driven by service progress.
- `internal/eats/controller.go`: storefront endpoints and bounded SSE writes.
- `internal/eats/kitchen.go`: cooking, deliberate oven fault, and queue retry.
- `internal/eats/service.go`: service-owned event subscriptions and delivery.
- `app/`, `app/kitchen/`, `app/delivery/`: ordinary App composition and lifecycle hooks.

These example files are created once. Rerendering preserves your changes.

## Local configuration

`EATS_KITCHEN_URL` can override the storefront's kitchen URL when deploying the
Apps separately. `EATS_GRAFANA_URL` controls browser trace links. For local pacing,
`EATS_STEP_DURATION` defaults to `1s` and accepts values from `10ms` to `5s`.
The scenic route triples each delivery step.

The demo uses in-memory order state and Core NATS. Orders and progress are
disposable across restarts. A shift accepts up to 100 orders; restart the Apps for
a fresh shift. The kitchen remembers injected oven faults separately from queue
metadata, so the deliberate failure happens once per order during that shift.

## Automated checks

The framework's dedicated Gopher Eats CI job renders this example, builds the
Vue UI, checks its committed assets, and starts race-enabled Go App binaries
against a real NATS broker. It verifies normal delivery, a failed cooking attempt
followed by a successful retry, SSE reconnects, cross-service trace parents, and
correlated OTLP logs.

For frontend history and Grafana-link checks:

```sh
cd cmd/app/frontend
node --test tests/eats-model.test.mjs
```

The checks cover reordered milestones, exact Grafana links, grouped activity
that preserves retries, and accessible Vue rendering of every kitchen stage.
